# Exercise: Make Your First Pull Request

**Level:** 1 — Learn GitHub  
**Estimated time:** 30–45 minutes  
**Prerequisites:** A GitHub account

## Goal

Make a change to your own copy of the Bharat AI Mission Contributor Practice repository and propose it to the original project through a pull request.

You will practise the workflow used by open-source contributors around the world.

## Step 1 — Fork the repository

Open the [Contributor Practice repository](https://github.com/bharataimission/contributor-practice).

1. Click **Fork** near the top of the page.
2. Select your personal GitHub account as the destination.
3. Create the fork.

A fork is your own copy of a repository where you can experiment without changing the original project.

## Step 2 — Create your own learner file

In your fork, open `learners/first-contribution.md`.

1. Copy the contents of the exercise template.
2. Return to the `learners` folder.
3. Choose **Add file → Create new file**.
4. Name the new file `learners/YOUR-GITHUB-USERNAME.md`, replacing the placeholder with your actual GitHub username.
5. Paste the template contents and complete the sections you are comfortable sharing.

**Important:** Create a new file rather than editing the original template.

## Step 3 — Create a branch

When saving your new file, select the option to create a new branch instead of committing directly to `main`.

Use a descriptive branch name, such as:

`first-contribution-your-username`

A branch allows you to develop a change independently before proposing it to the project.

## Step 4 — Commit your change

Use a clear commit message, for example:

`Add my first contributor profile`

A commit records the change you made.

## Step 5 — Open a pull request

In your fork, select **Contribute → Open pull request**, or use the pull-request interface to propose your branch to the original repository.

Check that:

- The base repository is `bharataimission/contributor-practice`.
- The base branch is `main`.
- Your head repository is your personal fork.
- Your proposed change adds your own learner file.

Give the pull request a descriptive title and explain what you added.

## Step 6 — Review your submission

Before submitting, inspect the **Files changed** tab.

Confirm that your pull request adds your own file and does not accidentally modify the original template or unrelated files.

Submit the pull request.

## Definition of done

- [ ] I forked the repository.
- [ ] I created a separate learner file.
- [ ] I used a branch for my change.
- [ ] I committed my change.
- [ ] I opened a pull request against the original repository.
- [ ] I checked the changes before submitting.

## What happens next?

A maintainer or mentor can review the pull request and provide feedback. In a later exercise, you will practise responding to review comments and updating your contribution.

**Congratulations!** You have practised the essential open-source contribution workflow: fork, branch, commit, and pull request.

You do not need to be a programmer to begin contributing to open source.
